# <%= current_page.data.title %>

> **This guidance is in preview and subject to change.** The versioned approach
> described here has not been fully agreed yet, although it is the direction we
> expect to adopt. Check back before making large-scale changes based on it.

Shared Terraform modules are versioned with semantic version Git tags of the
form `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` (for example `v2.0.1`).
[cpp-module-terraform-azurerm-key-vault](https://github.com/hmcts/cpp-module-terraform-azurerm-key-vault)
is the reference example of the full pattern — versioned releases, label-driven
release automation, and automated consumer updates.

## Consume a pinned version

Reference the module with an explicit `?ref=` pointing at a version tag:

```hcl
module "key_vault" {
  source = "git@github.com:hmcts/cpp-module-terraform-azurerm-key-vault.git?ref=v2.0.1"

  # ... see the module's variables.tf for the full list of inputs
}
```

Never point `?ref=` at a branch. A branch moves underneath every consumer with
no commit in the consuming repository to show it, and deleting the branch
breaks `terraform init` for everyone still referencing it. A tag is immutable:
what you plan against today is what you apply tomorrow.

## What the versions mean

| Segment | Increments when | Action for consumers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| MAJOR | A backwards-incompatible change: renamed or removed inputs or outputs, or behaviour that forces resource replacement | Review the release notes before upgrading; may need state changes |
| MINOR | New backwards-compatible functionality, such as a new optional input | Safe to adopt |
| PATCH | A backwards-compatible bug fix or maintenance change | Safe to adopt |

## Upgrade path

Renovate's Terraform manager detects new module tags and raises update pull
requests, so consumers do not need to watch module repositories. For the
key-vault module there is a ready-made preset,
[cpp-terraform-azurerm-key-vault](https://github.com/hmcts/.github/blob/master/renovate/cpp-terraform-azurerm-key-vault.json),
which automerges minor and patch updates once CI passes and raises major
updates as pull requests for review. See the [Renovate guide](renovate.html)
for how to enable it.

When taking a major update, read the release notes first — a major bump can
mean changed inputs or resource replacement, so always review the Terraform
plan before applying.

## Releasing a module

Module releases are automated with Release Drafter and pull request labels,
as described in the [release automation section](./#release-automation):

1. Every pull request must carry one of the standard labels
   (`breaking-change`, `enhancement`, `bug`, `dependencies`, `documentation`,
   `chore`) — enforced by the label check workflow.
2. On merge, Release Drafter creates or updates a draft release, resolving
   the next version from the labels of the merged pull requests.
3. Publishing the release creates the `vX.Y.Z` tag that consumers pin, and
   the changelog workflow records it in `CHANGELOG.md`.

The workflows are reusable — see the
[GitHub Actions library guide](/cloud-native-platform/shared-libraries/github-actions-library.html)
and the key-vault module's
[workflow definitions](https://github.com/hmcts/cpp-module-terraform-azurerm-key-vault/tree/main/.github/workflows)
for a working setup to copy.
